  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-3.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-3.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,-1),$ and  $(x,y)=(-1,2),$ so the global minimum points are $(1,-1,-3)$ and $(-1,2,-3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14132654/assets/7d62c7d1b3a265608fd93f395612092e1643858b1515f0e4d7a313b82b4c8f78.png)",
